{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"smashable-fameless-godwit","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Discovered from bug 9f63 (Step 7 parity sweep)\n\nBug 9f63 fixed the reconciler's cursor so the backfill path is sound for an **un-voted** change. This ticket is the parity gap it exposed: the **already-voted-but-wrong** case has no durable recovery at all.\n\n## The rule\n\n*A recovery path for a stuck Gerrit change must not permanently drop the request on a transient failure.*\n\n## Channel census\n\n| channel | holds? | citation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `POST /webhook` | holds (delegated to the reconciler) | `app.py:302-308` |\n| `_worker` timeout/exception | holds (no vote cast, so the reconciler re-drives) | `app.py:198-219` |\n| `reconcile_loop` | holds (fixed by 9f63) | `reconcile.py:230-236` |\n| **`POST /rerun`** | **MISSING** | `app.py:367-375` |\n| **`retrigger.handle_comment_added`** | **MISSING** | `retrigger.py:344-361` |\n| CLI | MISSING (no review-bot CLI surface exists at all) | — |\n\n## Why the reconciler is not a backstop for these two\n\nBoth `put_nowait` onto the in-memory `asyncio.Queue` (`app.py:112`) and return immediately. A container recreation — or the drain window at `app.py:143` elapsing — abandons whatever is still queued, leaving **zero trace**: no dedup row, no journald marker, no alert.\n\nThe reconciler cannot recover them, because its pre-filter skips any change that already carries a vote (`reconcile.py:261` `store.already_voted`, `:264` `has_llm_review_vote`). And a change stuck at a fail-closed `-1` is precisely the documented `/rerun` use case (`infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md:90,100`). So the one path that needs a backstop is the one the backstop excludes.\n\n`retrigger` is worse than `/rerun`: by the time the event can evaporate it has **already** burned the 0347 retry-budget re-arm (`retrigger.py:344` `reset_attempts`) and **already** posted a public reply telling the contributor \"a fresh review has been queued\" (`retrigger.py:358`). The contributor gets an affirmative acknowledgement for work that may never happen, and no signal to retry.\n\n## Fix sketch\n\nA durable `forced_pending` record alongside `voted` / `review_attempts` in `dedup.py:29-45`, written when a forced re-review is accepted and cleared when the resulting vote lands. `reconcile_once` consults it as an additional candidate source, so a forced re-review survives a restart even though the change already has a vote. One table, one extra loop.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An accepted `/rerun` survives a receiver restart before the worker dequeues it, proven by a test that enqueues, restarts, and asserts the review is re-driven\n- [ ] An accepted `recheck-review` survives the same restart, and the budget re-arm is not burned unless the request is durably recorded first\n- [ ] The reconciler re-drives a durably-recorded forced re-review even though the change already carries a vote\n- [ ] An abandoned forced re-review is observable rather than silent","id":"b0f5-eb05-ed27-4671","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"/rerun and recheck-review are fire-and-forget into a volatile queue, and the reconciler cannot back them up because the change already has a vote"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786121588168390001,"uuid":"03a11a60-3c3f-48e6-9a8e-209fcbe98e09"}